1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bengt_Birgersson
2 CONT
2 CONT Bengt Birgersson (1254 – 25 May 1291) was the youngest son of Birger Jarl. He embarked on an ecclesiastical career, becoming the Archdeacon of Linköping in 1273, the Canon of Uppsala in 1275, and the Bishop of Linköping in 1286. W
2 CONC hen open war broke out between his brothers, King Valdemar and Duke Magnus, Bengt sided with Magnus. After Magnus became King of Sweden in 1275, Bengt served as his chancellor. In 1284, he was granted the title of Duke of Finland.
2 CONC [1][2][3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Bengt Birgersson
2 CONT Duke of Finland
2 CONT Bishop of Linköping
2 CONT
2 CONT Born
2 CONT 1254
2 CONT Died
2 CONT 25 May 1291 (aged 36–37)
2 CONT Father
2 CONT Birger Jarl
2 CONT Mother
2 CONT Ingeborg Eriksdotter of Sweden
2 CONT Religion
2 CONT Roman Catholicism
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT edit
2 CONT Bengt Birgersson was a member of the House of Bjälbo (Folkungaätten).[4] He was the youngest son of Birger Magnusson (Birger Jarl), de facto ruler of Sweden from 1250 to 1266. His mother was Princess Ingeborg of Sweden, daughter o
2 CONC f Eric X of Sweden and sister of King Eric XI of Sweden.[5] [6][7][8] Two of his brothers, Valdemar and Magnus III, later became kings of Sweden. [9][10]
2 CONT
2 CONT Career
2 CONT edit
2 CONT He pursued an ecclesiastical career. While he was Archdeacon of Linköping Cathedral, he became chancellor to his brother, King Magnus. In 1284, some time after the death of his brother Eric, and during the reign of Magnus, he was 
2 CONC made Duke of Finland. He was the first known holder of that title. It is believed that the title did not include any fief. He is not known to have ever visited Finland, although he may have had responsibilities there related to th
2 CONC e defence of Sweden's eastern borderlands.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1286 he was elected Bishop of Linköping. Linköping's chronicle of bishops from 1523 tells of him: "Scriptores rerum suecicarum medii ævi". There exist at least two of his wills, from 1287 and 1289. When King Magnus died in 1290
2 CONC , Bengt assumed a role in the regency of the underage King Birger. However, he died from the plague just a half year later. He is buried in Linköping Catheral.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://wadbring.com/historia/sidor/bjalboaetten.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Bengt Birgersson (Folkungaätten)==
2 CONT Folkungaättens lagmans gren
2 CONT
2 CONT Biskop Bengt Birgersson (Folkungaätten)<br/>
2 CONT Hertig av Finland <br/>
2 CONT Kyrka Romersk-katolska kyrkan <br/>
2 CONT Stift Linköpings stift, biskop, Period 1286-1291 
2 CONT * Företrädare Bo (biskop)  
2 CONT * Efterträdare Lars Albrektsson 
2 CONT
2 CONT Född 1254. Bengt Birgersson död i pesten 25 maj 1291, biskop i Linköping. Son till Birger jarl och Ingeborg Eriksdotter. Bengt var först ärkedjäkne vid domkyrkan i Linköping och kansler till sin bror, Magnus Ladulås. 1284 upphöjde
2 CONC s han till hertig av Finland. 1286 valdes han till biskop i Linköpings stift.
2 CONT
2 CONT I Linköpings biskopskrönika från 1523 (Scriptores rerum suecicarum medii ævi) får han eftermälet: "til Linköpingz kirkio sent komber hans like. Misskunne honom Gud i hemmerijke."
2 CONT
2 CONT Bevarat finns minst två av hans testamenten, från 1287 och 1289. 1291 avled han i pesten.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bengt var först ärkedjäkne vid domkyrkan i Linköping och kansler till sin bror, Magnus Ladulås. 1284 upphöjdes han till hertig av Finland. 1286 valdes han till biskop i Linköpings stift.
2 CONT
2 CONT I Linköpings biskopskrönika från 1523 (Scriptores rerum suecicarum medii ævi) får han eftermälet: "til Linköpingz kirkio sent komber hans like. Misskunne honom Gud i hemmerijke."
2 CONT
2 CONT Bevarat finns minst två av hans testamenten, från 1287 och 1289.
