1 NAME William /Cole/
2 GIVN William
2 SURN Cole
2 _MARNM Cole
2 NICK Coale
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1598
2 ADDR
3 CITY Tillingham
3 STAE England
1 DEAT
2 DATE 15 SEP 1669
2 ADDR
3 CITY St. Jerome's
3 STAE Province of Maryland
1 OCCU House of Burgesses, (Maryland Immigrant), Nutmeg Quarter 1629
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} This William immigrated in 1618 on the Neptune and settled in Virginia.  All of his children were born in Virginia. Being a Quaker by faith, when the religious controls became strict in VA in 1650-51 he moved his f
2 CONC amily to St. Mary's County, Maryland.  '''So this family was of Virginia AND Maryland.''' (Details below.)
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a controversy over whether William ever had a wife named Frances. (Details below.)
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the will of William Coale, he was the father of two daughters, Sarah and Mary and four boys, Richard, William, John and Nicholas.[13]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT https://www.google.com/url?sa=t&rct=j&q=&esrc=s&source=web&cd=18&cad=rja&uact=8&ved=2ahUKEwja6JjBrennAhWxkHIEHSJ6BLI4ChAWMAd6BAgEEAE&url=http%3A%2F%2Fedlers.org%2Fpers%2FAP%2F06bcole.pdf&usg=AOvVaw1qUMyq1rVQYT5-mgwkrhu2<br/>
2 CONT (document attached https://www.geni.com/documents/view/6000000009687285398?doc_id=6000000124236586380) page 2
2 CONT
2 CONT WILLIAM COLE (b c1598) and SARAH [COLE] and their descendants
2 CONT
2 CONT William's parents were Humphrey Cole and Mary Mott<br/>
2 CONT Sarah's parents and birthdate are not known to us<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT A.  William     2      Cole     was born about 1598 Tillingham, Essex.  He immigrated to America about 1618 on the Neptune and settled in Warwick River Co VA (later Warwick Co).  He married Sarah [Cole]; probably at least six chil
2 CONC dren. In 1642 William bought 100 acres in Accawmack Co VA (Northamption Co after 1643), and later moved his family to St Mary's Co MD. He died about 1669 St Jerome, St Mary's Co, age about 71.  His will, dated 25 Mar 1659, was pro
2 CONC ved 17 Jul 1669 St Mary's Co, with his widow Sarah as executrix.<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT Earlier we thought, as do many researchers, that William first married a woman named Francis, who  immigrated to America in 1616 on the Susan.  '''The passenger list of the Susan, however, shows Francis as male, probably a kinsman
2 CONC  of William's.'''    While it is possible William did have an unidentified first wife, we now believe that '''the story of his marrying Francis is most likely a misunderstanding of the passenger list.'''
2 CONT * 1.  William 3  Coale, born about 1632 in VA, married Hester [Cole], Hannah [Coale], and Elizabeth Thomas                                                                                                        -- see page 6b-4. 
2 CONT * 2. John     3      Cole     was born in VA about 1634.  He probably married (--?--) [Cole] before 1655; at least one child.  He married Mary Beedle about 1663; probably at least two or three children.  We don't know birth order 
2 CONC of all the children.  Beginning in 1672, John was a tavern keeper in Townfield, Northampton Co, on the Eastern Shore of VA.  He died about 1705, age about 71.  We don't know anything about the Beedle family.
2 CONT * * a.  Richard 4  Cole was born about 1655. 
2 CONT * * b.  William 4  Cole. 
2 CONT * * c.  Robert 4  Cole. 
2 CONT * * d.  John     4      Cole    , born about 1669, married Johanna     Garrett     and Diana Hawkins      -- see page 6b-5.  
2 CONT * * e.  Frances 4  Cole married John Doe. 24
2 CONT * 3.  Sarah 3  Cole, born in VA, married Elias Beech about 1659 St Mary's Co MD.  She died before 1696. Born before 1634, he died 17 Feb 1696/97, age at least 62.
2 CONT * 4.  Richard 3  Cole died about 1677 St Mary's Co.
2 CONT * 5.  Nicholas 3  Cole died 1686 St Mary's Co.
2 CONT * 6.  Mary 3  Cole
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT From Hotten book from the '''Musters of the Inhabitants in Virginia 1624/1625''' chapters, pages 201 thru 265, which lists the muster captain, and what ship the individual arrived on.<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT Cole, William, 1618 voyage,  aged 26 at muster, Elizabeth City,  listed with '''Francis Cole''' and Roger Farbrase.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT '''First settled in Virginia, moved to Marylnd in 1650-51.''' (see below)<br/>
2 CONT https://www.facebook.com/ColeFamilyLineage/posts/william-cole-i1598-1664the-first-cole-immigrant-to-the-new-colonieshe-changed-hi/1124046744337329/<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT William Cole I was the first Cole ancestor in our line to migrate to America. He was born in 1598 in Essex County, England to Humphrey and Mary Mott Cole.He migrated to America in 1618 on a ship called the Neptune and landed in El
2 CONC izabeth City, Norfolk Co., VA.<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1618 to 1625 he worked as a farmer in Elizabeth City. In 1625 he obtained an early land grant of 50 acres. In 1628 he moved to the north side of the James River in Warwick Co., VA. He was elected representative for "Nutmeg Qu
2 CONC arter" in the assembly that met at Jamestown.<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1631 he had moved to the south bank of the James River near the Jamestown-Puritan area.<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1633 he married a girl named Sarah while living in VA and began his family. Being a Quaker by faith, when the religious controls became strict in VA, '''he moved his family in 1650 or 1651 to St. Jerones in southeastern St. Mar
2 CONC y's Co., MD''' on a farm with 400 acres.<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT All of William Cole's children were born in VA, but the young Friends (Quakers) were moving north along the western shore of the Chesapeake Bay.<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1657 his son, William Cole II, moved to Severn about 10 miles from Baltimore, MD.<br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1662 William Cole I, acquired 550 acres in Somerset Co. on the eastern shore of Chesapeake Bay.Whatever happened to this venture is unknown.Although William visited his children and grandchildren up north, he died at his home i
2 CONC n St. Jerones, St. Mary's Co., MD in 1669.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: http://www.sibertancestry.org/Alphabet/C/Coale/Coale.htm https://books.google.com/books?id=555CzPsGLDMC&pg=PA52&lpg=PA52&dq=william+coale+1598&source=bl&ots=WOtPDOAJV3&sig=ACfU3U2Yt0AZepDuxkUeVcx6BqLHYPM9qQ&hl=en&sa=X&ved
2 CONC =2ahUKEwja6JjBrennAhWxkHIEHSJ6BLI4ChDoATABegQICBAB#v=onepage&q=william%20coale%201598&f=false https://homepages.rootsweb.com/~marshall/esmd231.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''William Cole
2 CONT *'''M, #22013, b. circa 1618, d. before 15 September 1664
2 CONT *Father	Humphrie Cole b. c 1596, d. May 1624
2 CONT *Mother	Hester
2 CONT *'''     William Cole married Frances. William Cole was born circa 1618. He died before 15 September 1664 at Warwick Co., VA.
2 CONT *'''Family     Frances''' b. c 1597
2 CONT *'''Child	
2 CONT **William Cole+ b. c 1639, d. 4 Mar 1694
2 CONT *From: http://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p733.htm#i22013 ___________
2 CONT
2 CONT Emigration 1618 from Essex Co. England to Virginia, aboard the "Neptune". 
2 CONT
2 CONT Member, Virginia House of Burgesses. Elected 1629 Nutmeg Quarter Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT William Cole I was the first Cole ancestor in our line to migrate to America. He was born in 1598 in Essex County, England to Humphrey and Mary Mott Cole. He migrated to America in 1618 on a ship called the Neptune and landedin El
2 CONC izabeth City, Norfolk Co., VA. From 1618 to 1625 he worked as a farmer in Elizabeth City. In 1625 he obtained an early land grant of 50 acres. In 1628 he moved to the north side of the James River in Warwick Co., VA. He was electe
2 CONC d representative for "Nutmeg Quarter" in the assembly that met at Jamestown. By 1631 he had moved to the south bank of the James River near the Jamestown-Puritan area. In 1633 he married a girl named Sarah while living in VA and b
2 CONC egan his family.Being a Quaker by faith, when the religious controls became strict in VA, he moved his family in 1650 or 1651 to St. Jerones in southeastern St. Mary's Co., MD on a farm with 400 acres. All of William Cole's childr
2 CONC en were born in VA, but the young Friends (Quakers) were moving north along the western shore of the Chesapeake Bay. In 1657 his son, William Cole II, moved to Severn about 10 miles from Baltimore, MD. In 1662 William Cole I, acqu
2 CONC ired 550 acres in Somerset Co. on the eastern shore of Chesapeake Bay. Whatever happened to this venture is unknown. Although William visited his children and grandchildren up north, he died at his home in St. Jerones, St. Mary's 
2 CONC Co., MD in 1669.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: 1.Abbrev: Ball>Crawford>Esau>Legg>Woody> Carmen Johnson Title: Ball>Crawford>Esau>Legg>Woody> Carmen Johnson Author: Carmen Hope Esau Johnson Repository:  Name: Carmen Hope Johnson Po Box-545 Hoxie, KS 67740-0545 USA
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT William Cole
2 CONT * MyHeritage Family Trees
2 CONT * Birth:	1598 - Tillingham, Essex, England
2 CONT * Death:	Sep 15 1664 - Warwick County, Virginia Colony
2 CONT * Parents:	Humphrey Cole, Mary Cole (born Mott)
2 CONT * Siblings:	Samuel Cole, Martha Wood (born Cole), Robert Cole, Thomas Cole, John Cole, Mary Jane George (born Cole), Ann Jane Cole, Mary Jane Cole, Sister Cole
2 CONT ----
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Cole-70<br/>
2 CONT 1650 Migration to Maryland
2 CONT
2 CONT Being a Quaker by faith, when the religious controls became overly strict in Virginia, he moved his family in 1650 or 1651 to St. Jerones in southeastern St. Mary's County, MD on a farm with 400 acres.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, because of growing discontent in the community with a new royal governor, who insisted on suppressing old prized liberties, including that of free self-government, William decided to move his family to lands of greater op
2 CONC portunity. He decided to relocate north along the Chesapeake Bay to a newly established province called Maryland. During the winter of 1650-51, in company with other Virginia emigrants, '''William took his family to Maryland'''. I
2 CONC n his new country, William would be over 50, and devoting his old pioneer energy to the development of 400 acres of raw wilderness land. This was the size of the grant claimed for him as an immigrant settler with family. To help h
2 CONC im at the start , he had one contract servant and probably a son nearly grown. This is supported via "demand" land patent, dated 24 Mar 165?, [Patent Record Book AB & H, p. 201, SR 7344, Maryland State Archives -- Land Office] [7]
2 CONT
2 CONT The location was St. Jerome, on the lower western shore of St. Marys Co. Here community life would be with neighbors who also were former Virginians. The capital of St. Marys was only a few miles away.
2 CONT
2 CONT (More details at the wikitree link.)
