1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.encyclopediavirginia.org/Cole_William_1638_or_1639-1694
2 CONT
2 CONT William Cole served as a member of the governor's Council (1675–1692) and as secretary of the colony (1690–1692). Cole served as an intermediary between Nathaniel Bacon and Governor Sir William Berkeley during the early months of 
2 CONC Bacon's Rebellion (1676–1677), but ultimately sided with the latter. As a councilor, Cole pushed for stronger governmental control on trade and helped crack down on piracy. Cole resigned as councilor, secretary, and customs collec
2 CONC tor in 1692, after Lieutenant Governor Francis Nicholson confronted him about a letter Cole wrote to Governor Francis Howard, baron Howard of Effingham, disparaging Nicholson. Despite Cole's fall from power, he was the second man 
2 CONC named in the College of William and Mary's charter and a founding trustee of the institution. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''William Cole
2 CONT *'''M, #21854, b. circa 1639, d. 4 March 1694
2 CONT *Father	William Cole b. c 1618, d. b 15 Sep 1664
2 CONT *Mother	Frances b. c 1597
2 CONT *'''     William Cole was born circa 1639. He married Susan Croft''', daughter of Richard Croft, '''before 1674. William Cole married Ann Digges''', daughter of Edward Digges, Esq., Governor of Virginia and Elizabeth Page, '''circ
2 CONC a 1681.1 William Cole died on 4 March 1694.1
2 CONT *'''Family 1     Susan Croft
2 CONT *'''Child	
2 CONT **Susannah Cole+ b. c 1675, d. 21 Nov 1708
2 CONT *'''Family 2     Ann Digges''' b. c 1658, d. 22 Nov 1686
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT *[S61] Unknown author, Family Group Sheets, Family History Archives, SLC.
2 CONT *From: http://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p728.htm#i21854 _______________
2 CONT *'''William Cole
2 CONT *'''Birth: 	1638
2 CONT *'''Death: 	Mar. 4, 1694
2 CONT *'''William Cole (1638-1694) served as an attorney in Warwick County and commanded the county's militia. He was a member of the Council of the State from 1674 until 1692. 
2 CONT *'''During Bacon's Rebellion, Cole negotiated unsuccessfully with Nathaniel Bacon to end the conflict and later fled with Governor Berkley to the Eastern Shore. After Bacon's death, Cole resumed his political career, serving as th
2 CONC e colonial secretary of state from 1689 to 1692 and as a trustee for the College of William and Mary. He also acquired more land in York County, Elizabeth City County, and Warwick County. 
2 CONT *'''Cole left his heirs more than 3,600 acres of land and a rich political legacy. His son''', Colonel William Cole (1692-1729), inherited Balthrope and served as a member of the House of Burgesses as well as a Warwick County just
2 CONC ice, sheriff, and militia commander.
2 CONT *The manor house remained in Cole family ownership until 1766. The Boldrup Plantation Archaeological Site is listed in the Virginia Landmarks Register and the National Register of Historic Places.
2 CONT *http://www.flickr.com/photos/nostri-imago/3448344543/
2 CONT * **bio provided by Michael Lustig, michael@lustigworld.com
2 CONT *'''His children by Martha Cole (source - Ancestry.com)
2 CONT **John Cole
2 CONT **1686 – 1698
2 CONT **Martha Cole
2 CONT **1690 – 1698
2 CONT **Col. William Cole
2 CONT **1692 – 1729
2 CONT **Mary Cole
2 CONT **1694 – 1698
2 CONT *Family links: 
2 CONT *''' Spouse:
2 CONT *'''  Martha Yeo Lear Burwell (1668 - 1704)*
2 CONT *''' Children:
2 CONT **  Susannah Cole Digges (1674 - 1708)*
2 CONT **  Martha Cole (1690 - 1698)*
2 CONT *Burial:  Cole Family Graveyard, Newport News City, Virginia, USA
2 CONT *Find A Grave Memorial# 20183415
2 CONT *From: http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=20183415 __________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Appears to be first Cole born in the New World;  SEE ATTACHED ARTICLE IN MEDIA SECTION
2 CONT
2 CONT Successful; landowner; atty; 
2 CONT
2 CONT Royal apt as Virginia's Secretary of State in 1689
2 CONT
2 CONT Tipton etc descended from his third wife
2 CONT
2 CONT Cole received a royal appointment as Virginia's secretary of state in the fall of 1689 and served in that capacity for almost three years, tendering his resignation because he was "lately much decayed in body" and suffering from "
2 CONC deepe Melancholly." At the same time, Cole gave up his office as collector of the Lower District of the James River. Cole was among the colonists who petitioned the Crown for the establishment of a college in the colony, and when 
2 CONC the College of William and Mary was formed in 1692, Cole was named one of its first trustees.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Cole married at least three times, first in 1674 (wife unknown), again in 1680, to Ann Diggs''' (c.1657–1686), and '''after Ann's death, to Martha Lear''' (1668–1704), the daughter of fellow Councilor John Lear, '''by whom Cole
2 CONC  had a son''', William Cole, III (d. 1729), a burgess in the eighteenth century. Boldrup was the home of Cole and his descendents for about a century, but all that remains of the estate now are some brick foundations, archaeologic
2 CONC al remains, and a gravesite. '''The cemetery contains the graves of Cole and two of his wives; his tombstone, inscribed with the family coat of arms, reads in part''', "Of him this may be loudly sounded far He was unspotted on ye 
2 CONC bench untaynted at ye bar." A dig conducted by the James River Institute for Archaeology in the late 1980s revealed earlier English occupation of Boldrup from about 1636 to 1650. '''Cole patented a tract of 618 acres in York Count
2 CONC y in 1683, and two years later he purchased another 1,433 acres lying in both Warwick and Elizabeth City Counties, the site of the original settlement of Newport News.
2 CONT
2 CONT References: Donald A. D'Amato. 1992. Warwick's 350-Year Heritage: A Pictorial History. Virginia Beach, VA: Donning. Edward M. Riley and Charles E. Hatch, Jr., eds. 1946. James Towne In the Words of Contemporaries. Washington, DC: 
2 CONC National Park Service.http://trees.ancestry.com/tree/6071592/person/967483253/story/f8c43dee-d0dc-4d68-9178-6432607e68b7?otid=20741388&opid=20419127639&pg=32799%2c32842&pgpl=pid
2 CONT
2 CONT SECRETARY OF STATE.--Lord Culpeper, writing in 1683, said, The Secretary is a patent officer, from the first seating of the country, the very next in dignity to the Governor, or Commander-in-Chief." The office was one which confer
2 CONC red much power and influence on the occupant. He had the right to appoint all county clerks, and as these were men of weight in their respective communities, it was frequently charged that through them the Secretary exercised too 
2 CONC much influence in the House of Burgesses. He was keeper of the colonial seal and ex-offio clerk of the Council and General Court, though the duties of these offices were actually performed by the titular clerks of the respective b
2 CONC odies.
2 CONT
2 CONT All patents and other papers from the executive were issued from the Secretary's office, and all of the executive records, as well as those of the General Court were in his custody.
2 CONT
2 CONT An important part of the Secretary's duties was to keep the English govemment constantly informed in regard to affairs in Virginia, and send home copies of all public papers. http://trees.ancestry.com/tree/6071592/person/967483253
2 CONC /story/74f86fcc-3725-4a18-be22-37c37fa1b928?otid=20741388&opid=20419127639&pg=32799%2c32842&pgpl=pid#Current
2 CONT
2 CONT __________________________
