1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Holger Jørgensen Rosenkrantz=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Jørgen Ottesen Rosenkrantz and Dorthe Gundesdatter Lange
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =Married= 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Married: '''Sophie Axelsdatter Brahe''' on 13-08-1598 in Malmö Sweden. The wedding took place at Malmøhus Sweden,  where her sisters Birgitte and Anne were simultaneously married to Henrik Gøye and Eiler Qvitzow, respectively. I
2 CONC n 1598, he married Sophie Brahe (1578–1646) and they had 13 children, eight of whom survived to adulthood. Upon his father's death, Rosenkrantz inherited Rosenholm Castle and following his mother's death in 1613, he inherited Skaf
2 CONC øgård. In 1627, he withdrew from political life and devoted himself to theological writing. His commitment to theology and education became so ardent that he relinquished his fief and retired to Rosenholm, where he fully immersed 
2 CONC himself in his studies and Christian life. He maintained correspondence with theologians across Europe
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =History=  
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1617, King Christian IV appointed Holger Rosenkrantz as a councillor of the realm. Rosenkrantz played an important role in the king's restoration and reform of the school system in Denmark.
2 CONT
2 CONT The academy, as it was later called, was founded by Holger Jørgensen Rosenkrantz (1574-1642) with the nickname 'the learned'. Rosenkrantz was an internationally recognized theologian, and Rosenholm can therefore be compared to Tyc
2 CONC ho Brahe's (1546-1601) natural science academy at Uranienborg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Holger Rosenkrantz (December 14, 1574 at Kalø Castle – October 28, 1642 in Copenhagen) was a Danish nobleman, councillor of the realm, theologian and educator. He is considered one of the wisest Danes of the 17th century, and was 
2 CONC known as "the learned Holger". Holger Rosenkrantz to Rosenholm and Øster Vallø.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
