1 NOTE {geni:about_me} John of Lüneburg (c. 1242 – 13 December 1277) was a German duke. He and his brother Albert were jointly the second Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg until the partition of the duchy, and John was the first ruler of the ne
2 CONC wly-created Principality of Lüneburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Life
2 CONT
2 CONT John's father, Otto I, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg, was the first Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg. After his death in 1252, John ruled the duchy jointly with his elder brother Albert. As the brothers could not agree on how to govern the
2 CONC  duchy, in 1267 they decided to divide their possession. In 1269 John received the right to choose his part. He chose the part containing the cities of Lüneburg and Hanover, forming the Principality of Lüneburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT John founded the First (or Elder) House of Lüneburg. He was buried in the cloister of St. Michaelis in Lüneburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Family and children
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1265 John married Liutgard of Holstein (d. after 28 February 1289), daughter of Count Gerhard I of Holstein-Itzehoe, and had five children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke Otto II of Lüneburg (d. 9 April 1330) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Matilde (d. after 1308), married in 1291 to Henry I, Prince of Mecklenburg-Güstrow 
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth (d. 1294/1298), married to Count John II of Oldenburg 
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes, married c. 1283 to Werner I von Hadmersleben 
2 CONT
2 CONT Helene, married in 1315 to Count Conrad III of Wernigerode 
2 CONT
2 CONT His illegitimate son, Heinrich von Braunschweig (d. 23 August 1324), was a canon in Walsrode.
