1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Charles VIII of Sweden, Charles I of Norway==
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_VIII_of_Sweden '''Charles VIII of Sweden'''] (1408 Uppsala - 1470 Stockholm, in reality Charles II), '''Charles I of Norway''', also Carl (Swedish: Karl Knutsson), was king of Sweden (1448–14
2 CONC 57, 1464–1465 and from 1467 to his death in 1470) and king of Norway (1449–1450).
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Regnal name===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Charles was the''' second Swedish king by the name of Charles (Karl). Charles VIII is a posthumous invention, counting backwards from Charles IX (r. 1604–1611) who adopted his numeral according to a fictitious history of Sweden
2 CONC . Six others before Charles VII are unknown to any sources before Johannes Magnus's 16th century book Historia de omnibus gothorum sueonumque regibus, and are considered his invention. Charles was the first Swedish monarch of the 
2 CONC name to actually use a regnal number as Charles II (later retrospectively renumbered VIII), on his queen's tombstone (1451) at Vadstena.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Early life===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Karl Knutsson was''' born in October 1408 or 1409, at Ekholmen Castle, the son of Knut Tordsson (Bonde), knight and member of the privy council (riksråd), and Margareta Karlsdotter (Sparre av Tofta), the only daughter and heire
2 CONC ss of Charles Ulvsson, Lord of Tofta. His father Knut was first cousin of Erik Johansson Vasa's father. King Charles died 14 May 1470. His first marriage, in 1428, to Birgitta Turesdotter (Bielke) (died 1436) gave him his daughter
2 CONC  Kristina. His second marriage, in 1438, to Catherine (Gumsehuvud, died in 1450) produced his second daughter Magdalena, who married Ivar Axelsson (Tott). He also had two children by his third wife (and former mistress) Christina 
2 CONC Abrahamsdotter, Anna and Karl. His father was said by contemporary legends to descend from a younger brother of King Eric IX (Saint Eric). His mother, an important heiress, descended from Jarl Charles the Deaf and consequently fro
2 CONC m some ancient Folkunge earls of Sweden, as well as from Ingegerd Knutsdotter, a daughter of Canute IV of Denmark and Adela of Flanders.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Growing influence===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In 1434 he became''' a member of the Privy Council of Sweden and in October of the same year he assumed one of its most senior offices, Lord High Constable of Sweden, or Riksmarsk. Because of the growing dissatisfaction with Ki
2 CONC ng Eric of Pomerania among the Swedish nobility, Charles was in 1436 made Rikshövitsman, an office equating to Military Governor of the Realm, and finally replaced the king as an elected regent from 1438 to 1440, as the result of 
2 CONC the rebellion by Engelbrekt Engelbrektsson. During Charles's brief regentship, the so-called Rebellion of David (a peasant rebellion) took place in Finland. Eric of Pomerania was forced to step down from the throne and in 1440 Chr
2 CONC istopher of Bavaria, was elected king of Sweden, Norway and Denmark. At the coronation of Christopher in September 1441, Charles was dubbed a knight and appointed Lord High Justiciar of Sweden, or Riksdrots. In October he resigned
2 CONC  as Lord High Justiciar and resumed his office as Lord High Constable. From 1442 he was the military governor, hövitsman, at Vyborg in Finland (margrave of Viborg).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Charles acquired extensive''' fiefs, for example in Western Finland. His first seat was in Turku. Soon, Christopher's government began to take back fiefs and positions and Charles was forced to give up the castle of Turku. Char
2 CONC les's next seat was the castle of Vyborg, on Finland's eastern border, where he kept an independent court, taking no heed of Christopher and exercising his own foreign policy in relation to such powers in the region as the Hanseat
2 CONC ic League, the Russian city of Novgorod and the Teutonic Knights in what are today Estonia and Latvia.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===King of Sweden===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''At the death of''' Christopher in 1448, without a direct heir, Charles was elected king of Sweden on 20 June and on 28 June he was hailed as the new monarch at the Stones of Mora, not far from Uppsala, mostly due to his own mil
2 CONC itary troops being present at the place, against the wishes of regents Bengt and Nils Jönsson (Oxenstierna). The Danish had in September 1448 elected Christian I as their new monarch. A rivalry ensued between Charles and Christian
2 CONC  for the throne of Norway, which had also been ruled by Christopher, with both kings gaining support from various factions in the Norwegian Council of the realm. In 1449 a portion of the Norwegian council elected Charles King of N
2 CONC orway, and he was crowned in Nidaros Cathedral in Trondheim on 20 November. However, Christian also continued pursuing his claim to Norway. The Swedish aristocracy was reluctant to back Charles in a war against Denmark over Norway
2 CONC , and already in 1450, Charles was forced to relinquish the throne of Norway in favour of Christian.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1451, Sweden and Denmark were in state of war against each other. Because of devastating warring, a growing opposition against Charles emerged among the nobility in Sweden. The strongest opponent was the Swedish church which 
2 CONC opposed Charles's efforts to concentrate royal and secular power. Other opponents were the family group of Oxenstierna and the House of Vasa, which had been on the opposing side in the election of king and lost.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Later reigns===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''During the next 20''' years, Charles was deposed twice, only to regain the throne and reign three times (1448–57, 1464–65, 1467–70).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In 1457, a rebellion''' took place, led by Archbishop Jöns Bengtsson (Oxenstierna) and a nobleman, Erik Axelsson Tott. Charles went into exile to Danzig (Gdańsk). The two leaders of the revolt took the regentship, and organized
2 CONC  the election of Christian I of Denmark as king (firstly in Turku, then in Stockholm).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In 1463, King Christian''' quarrelled with the Archbishop because of his taxation policies. The Archbishop was imprisoned, which resulted in a rebellion by his relatives, and led to Christian being driven out of Sweden. Charles
2 CONC  was recalled by the rebels and returned at the head of a force of German and Polish mercenaries. Upon arrival in Sweden he found himself at war with the Archbishop and after two bloody battles in the winter of 1464–1465 Charles w
2 CONC as again exiled. In 1467, the regent Erik Axelsson Tott, now having reverted to support Charles, once more had him crowned. Charles then reigned for three years, sharing power with the Riksråd, until his death in Stockholm in May 
2 CONC 1470.
2 CONT <br/>
2 CONT <br/>
