2 DATE 25 OCT 1437
2 ADDR
3 CTRY Denmark
1 OCCU Riksråd, häradshövding
1 TITL Lord of Vinstorp manor
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://reader.digitale-sammlungen.de/en/fs1/object/display/bsb10328239_00104.html
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.adelsvapen.com/genealogi/Vinstorpa%C3%A4tten
2 CONT
2 CONT Bengt Uddsson (död på vintern 1436 eller 1437), riksråd och häradshövding i Redvägs härad i Västergötland 1412 och riksråd 1435. Levde ännu 25.10 1436 men blev ihjälslagen i Västergötland under Erik Pukes (Rossviksätten) uppror. G
2 CONC ift med Kristina Staffansdotter, dotter till riksrådet och riddaren Staffan Ulfsson (Ulv) och hans andra hustru Iliana Åkesdotter (bååt). Bengt Uddsson hade bytt till sig gården Tummatorp i Dalums socken vid Vinstorpasjön mittemot
2 CONC  gamla Vinstorp och han flyttade sätesgården dit och gav den namnet Nya Vinstorp, vilket är nuvarande Vinsarp. 
2 CONT https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vinstorpa%C3%A4tten
2 CONT
2 CONT http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00490134&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.culturum.se/Mathis/1Winsarp.htm Vinsarp's stone house in Dalum parish dates back to the late 15th century, but the farm's history goes back more than a century in history. However, the old Vinstorpa family was not its s
2 CONC ole owner. They were not even the first in the estate's history.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vinsarp is located by Vinsarpssjön in Dalum parish. In Böne parish on the other side of the lake is today Gamlegården. From 1473 until 1659, these two farms go by the names Nya and Gamla Vinstorp and their history is common. The n
2 CONC ame Vinsarp only began to be used during the 16th century. During the 15th century, both farms were owned by the Vinstorpa family. New Vinstorp was created by Bengt Uddsson through farm exchanges with the farmers in Tummatorp. Thr
2 CONC ough this and several farm purchases, he became the owner of large parts of Dalum and Böne parishes.  Several Vinstorpa families
2 CONT
2 CONT The Vinstorpa family is a collective name for several savior families from 1250 until 1500. The connection between them is unclear except that they carried the same coat of arms. The peculiarity of the families is that the appeara
2 CONC nce of the varied coats of arms so that two contemporary people never carried the same weapon and that the weapon was often carried on the women's side as well. The latter makes it probable that it is the wives who bind the famili
2 CONC es together, even though we do not always know the women's names. In one case, even the son takes his mother's name, Magnus Kristinasson (so-called matronymic icon)
2 CONT
2 CONT Vinsarp was the manor house for the youngest branch, the actual Vinstorpa family, which is the only one we know for sure has owned land in the Redvägs district. The family is descended from Knut Abjörnsson who carried the Vinstorp
2 CONC a weapon. His daughter Ingeborg married Matts Oddsson. Their son Udd Mattson had Redvägs district with the city of Bogesund as a grant. He had four sons but only the youngest son Bengt passed on the family because none of the olde
2 CONC r ones, the rector Karl, Skarabiskopen Sigge, or the councilor Knut, had any children. Udd Mattson himself had a lily in his coat of arms, but his sons used his mother's wineglass coat of arms. However, Bengt brought his father's 
2 CONC lily for a short time.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1363, the year before Udd Mattson is in the sources, Odde Kraam appears in Bogesund. Both Odde Kraam and Udd Mattson's son Bengt owned a farm in Brismene parish, Frökinds district. Maybe these two can be one and the same person. S
2 CONC igns that indicate this is that they are in Sjuhärad at the same time and that both carry a lily weapon. Within the Kraam family, there is also the Vinstorpavapnet, which indicates a close connection between these two families. Is
2 CONC  it possible that what we describe as the actual Vinstorpa family is in fact the Kraam family?  Old Vinstorp
2 CONT
2 CONT Gamla Vinstorp was first mentioned as early as 1323 when Sigge Djäkn bought the farm from Haldor Kase. The farm was then inherited by Sigge's granddaughter Ingrid Karlsdotter (Lars Björnsson's family). Ingrid was in turn married t
2 CONC o Udd Mattson - the ancestor of the actual Vinstorpa family - and thus the farm has come into the Vinstorpa family's ownership. Udd Mattson also bought the farm Nyatorp in Böne parish.
2 CONT
2 CONT Haldor Kase's sale of the farm took place within the family. The son Olof Kase was married to Kristina Siggadotter (Djäkn), and together they had the children Magnus and Ragnhild. In the succession after Olof Kase it appeared that
2 CONC  he had, among others, the farms «Aslered», Björke, Böne, Hedetorp, Hulsjö, Hälle, Högelyckan, »Kakresta», Karistad, Knutsbo, Källestorp, Lafrinstorp, Lund (en), Lönnåsen, Svansö, Ubbared , «Warifskälf» and the mill stream in Nyda
2 CONC l. Erik Erlandsson Sparre and Knut Uddsson of the Vinstorpa family act as seals of the succession. On the same day as the succession is carried out, Magnus Kase mortgages three farms to his cousin Knut Uddsson.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is not impossible that most of these farms have been inherited or pledged to the Vinstorpa family. Anna Karlsdotters, the last of the Vinstorpa family, and her husband Erik Eriksson Gyllenstierna's granddaughter's husband, Clae
2 CONC s Åkesson Tott, owned Vinsarp as well as Böne and Ubbared in 1575. At least Svansö in Bottnaryd and Lund (en) in Knätte were bought after Magnus' death by Bengt Uddsson. The farm was originally owned by Sigge Djäkn, but went after
2 CONC  a change to Olof Kase.  New Vinstorp
2 CONT
2 CONT Riksrådet Bengt Uddsson - married to Kristina Staffansdotter (Ulv) - exchanged three tax farms in Tummatorp, on the other side of Vinsarpssjön, for a farm in Ubbatorp, one in Humla and one in Kölaby. All three were old salvation f
2 CONC arms. He fell during Erik Puke's rebellion against the head of state, Karl Knutsson Bonde, in the winter of 1436–37. The change of farm and the inheritance after Bengt Uddsson was recognized in 1461 at the court in «Nörene i Tymel
2 CONC le». The parliament confirmed that "the crown was genuine", ie. the krona had not incurred any loss through the exchange. Witnesses at the court were, in addition to his son Staffan Bengtsson, the Riksrådet, and the knight Gustav 
2 CONC Olofsson Stenbock (the older family - not from Torpa), as well as a rector named Olof Slampa. Gustav Stenbock was married to a sister of Staffan. It may happen that with the change, Bengt Uddsson moved his manor from Gamla Vinstor
2 CONC p to Nya Vinstorp already during the 1430s. Gamla Vinstorp's strategic location was not the best, and a change was justified. Both Nya and Gamla Vinstorp were inherited by their son Staffan Bengtsson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Old Vinstorp's heyday ends with the Riksråd and the knight Staffan Bengtsson. During the unrest after King Karl Knutsson's death in 1470, he stood on King Kristian I's side against Sten Sture d.ä. After the loss in the battle of Ö
2 CONC resten (Mark's district) in 1470, he was therefore forced to leave Vinstorp to his brother Karl. As a large landowner, Sten Sture understood d.ä. certainly the importance of not unnecessarily selling a family property to outsiders
2 CONC . In addition, Sten Sture d.ä. married Staffan's niece Ingeborg, and excessively harsh punishment would probably cause problems in family relationships. Staffan, however, fell already the following year in the battle of Brunkeberg
2 CONC , and in connection with the succession in 1472, Vinstorp returned to Staffan's daughter Mekthild. During his short time on the farm, Karl Bengtsson seems to have taken a liking to his childhood home district and had the farm chan
2 CONC ged to him again as early as 1473.
2 CONT
2 CONT No later than 1473, the three farms in Tummatorp were merged into one and renamed Nya Vinstorp. A distinction between Gamla and Nya Vinstorp was made in a letter in which Karl Bengtsson's ownership of both is confirmed. He then ex
2 CONC changed the farms for his niece Mekthild. The letter also emphasizes that Nya Vinstorp was formerly called Tummatorp. This village is not mentioned after 1482, which is why it is probable that at this time it was completely settle
2 CONC d in Nya Vinstorp. Karl Bengtsson moved his entire home here and had the stone house built. He thus left his old manor house Glaskulla in Vårkumla parish in Frökind district. Karl was a councilor in 1473, possibly as early as 1471
2 CONC , and probably took over his brother Staffan's place on the kingdom's board, but was himself a supporter of Sten Sture d.ä. and thus opponents of King Christian I.  Gamla anor
2 CONT
2 CONT According to tradition, Vinstorp is a farm dating back to ancient times, and its owners are powerful chiefs in the area. The name change -torp, however, indicates that the farm is a new building that was added during or after the 
2 CONC 12th century. Of Udd Mattsson's children, the youngest son Bengt inherited the farm, which suggests that its significance was small. The older brother Knut prefers to settle on his wife's manor Vänngarn in Uppland. As a goods comp
2 CONC lex, Vinstorp becomes interesting first with Bengt Uddsson's farm shops. With exchange, inheritance, and purchase, the farm grows to a significant size with direct ownership in two parishes around Vinsarpssjön. Probably the exchan
2 CONC ge of the farms in Tummatorp should be seen against this background. It was less a matter of finding the most strategic location for the farm than a concentration of estate holdings.
2 CONT
2 CONT As mentioned above, the Vinstorpa family is a collective name - given afterward - of several old families with the same coat of arms. The connection between these is partly unclear, but a relationship can be deduced from the desig
2 CONC n of the coats of arms. The family described above is the last and probably the only one that is rooted in the area.  Gyllenstierna
2 CONT
2 CONT The last of the Vinstorpa family - Anna Karlsdotter (see above), who among others. a. was accused of being the infamous «pintorpafrun» - married in 1468 to a cousin of Gustav Vasa's grandfather Erik Karlsson (died 1491) and in 149
2 CONC 2 to Erik Eriksson Gyllenstierna (died 1502).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Gyllenstierna family was one of Denmark's most powerful in the 15th century. It came to Sweden from Jutland in 1438 with Erik Eriksson d.ä. (later addition, after obtaining baronial dignity: af Lundholm). He accompanied the th
2 CONC en recurring king Karl Knutson Bonde's court and later married the king's daughter Kristina. (It may be added that the Vaasa family at this time had no higher rank.)
2 CONT
2 CONT He seems to have stood highly in favor of the king, for Erik was a very hired diplomat. In 1450 he was an envoy to the meeting with the Danes, in 1453 to King Kristian, and in 1468 and 1469 to the meeting with the Danes in Halmsta
2 CONC d. In 1471 he was part of the truce treaty between Kristian and the Swedish parliament and in 1472, -73, and -74 he participated in the peace negotiations with the Danes in Kalmar.
2 CONT
2 CONT Of his ten children (eight reached adulthood), three are particularly noted. Abraham became Danish councilor and courtier in Kalmar, Nils was a courtier in Öland and Viborg in 1481, and from 1482 until his death in 1485 Swedish co
2 CONC uncilor. The third Erik d.y. is the ancestor of the Vinsarps and Årås branches; he wrote to Fogelvik (Tryserums sn, Kalmar län) after his father and to Vinstorp after his wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nils married Sigrid Eskilsdotter Banér (Gustav Vasa's grandmother) and among several children had a daughter Kristina born in 1494. In 1511 Kristina married for the first time the Riksrådet - later the director of the Riksdag - St
2 CONC en Sture d.y.
2 CONT
2 CONT In her second marriage, with Johan Turesson Tre Rosor (Gustav Vasa's cousin), she had a son Gustav Johansson Tre Rosor (he and Kristina's son in the first marriage Svante Sture and Per Brahe, was appointed at Erik XIV's coronation
2 CONC  as Sweden's first three counts). According to legend, Gustav Johansson was secretly betrothed to Katarina Stenbock when Gustav Vasa took her to his third wife in 1552.  End of the heyday
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Karlsdotter was a very powerful and energetic woman and was, among other things. in inheritance disputes with Gustaf Vasa. She died in 1552 and is buried in Dalum's church.
2 CONT
2 CONT The son Carl Eriksson Gyllenstierna af Lundholm wrote to Vinsarp during the years 1502–41. He was married to Mariana Nilsdotter Grip to Nynäs. He was councilor, Lagman in Västergötland and Dal, governor of Kalmar m.m. At Gustaf Va
2 CONC sa's coronation in 1528, he was appointed a knight.
2 CONT
2 CONT His son Erik Carlsson Gyllenstierna af Lundholm (1541–1586) then wrote to Vinsarp. He is a counselor, a lawyer in Småland, a member of the castle court in Stockholm, and much more.
2 CONT
2 CONT By exchange with Duke Charles (later Charles IX), he acquired Årås in Kölingared in 1573; subsequent year breeding farm; 1576 his manor; 1574 disposed of Vinsarp.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is buried in Kölingared's church - his tombstone is considered the most important in Västergötland from the Vasa Renaissance. The choice of the cemetery indicates that he actually stayed in the area.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vinsarp then passed to Erik Karlsson's niece Elin Klasdotter Tott, married to Hans Eriksson Ulfsparre af Broxvik.  Why leave Vinstorp?
2 CONT
2 CONT How can it be that you abandon an estate and move to another place? In some writings, it has been said that the Danes after the battle of Bogesund 1520, where Sten Sture d.y. was killed (he who was married to Kristina Gyllenstiern
2 CONC a), moved on along Ätran and burned Vinsarp. However, there is no indication that any ravages took place then.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Nordic Seven Years' War of 1563–70, the Danes were also in the area and it is probable that Vinsarp was burned then. (This is the last time a foreign army penetrated far into the country; just a small parenthesis.)
2 CONT
2 CONT But if a house burns down, you do not abandon your yard for it. It is the land that has economic significance. Something else must therefore be behind it.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vinsarp's heyday is therefore before the 1570s. Then the power - and Gyllenstierna - moves to Årås. Vinsarp will soon lose its rights as a manor. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://runeberg.org/nfcl/0385.html
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Bengt Uddsson''' av Vinstorpaätten. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Född i Böne, Vinstorp. Riksråd i Vinsarp, Dalum. Omnämnd 1407. Häradshövding i Redvägs härad i Västergötland, 1412 och ännu 1424. Blev riksråd 1435. Gift före 1418. Ägde gods i stora delar av södra Sverige. Nya Vinsatorp Riksrådet
2 CONC  Bengt Uddsson bytte till sig tre skattegårdar i Tummatorp, på Vinsarpssjöns andra sida, mot en gård i Ubbatorp, en i Humla och en i Kölaby. Alla dessa tre var gamla frälsegårdar. Han stupade under Erik Pukes uppror mot riksförest
2 CONC åndaren Karl Knutsson Bonde vintern 1436-37. Gårdsbytet och arvet efter Bengt Uddsson erkändes 1461 vid tinget i "Nörene i Tymelle" (dagens Nöre by, mellan Dalum och Timmele). Tinget bekräftade att kronan var osviken, det vill säg
2 CONC a att kronan inte hade åsamkats någon förlust genom bytet. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nya Vinstorp skapades av Bengt Uddsson genom gårdsbyten med bönderna i Tummatorp. Genom detta och ett flertal gårdsköp blev han ägare till stora delar av Dalums och Böne socknar, på Vinsarpssjöns andra sida, mot en gård i Ubbatorp
2 CONC , en i Humla och en i Kölaby. Alla dessa tre var gamla frälsegårdar.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Riddare,Riksråd.nämns1407-1436 källa Theutenbergs "Mellan liljan och sjöbladet"
